  "recent_corrections": [
    {
      "ts": "2026-09-09T12:04:21.214404+00:00",
      "text": "Two more WATCH-level flags were wrong, both for the same reason. On 7 September a question-framed analysis piece about hybrid warfare, and on 9 September a television studio conversation about domestic tax policy, were each flagged as Baltic security signals. Neither reported that anything had happened. Cause: headlines phrased as a question are already treated as opinion rather than reporting, but that check only looked for a question mark at the very end of the headline. Both of these asked their question in the middle and then added a few words promising the answer, and went straight through. The rule now covers that shape too. Checked against every alert this project has published: three items are newly caught and all three were noise; no genuine alert is affected. Logged here without a separate notification, since the earlier correction today already went out to subscribers.",
      "context": "WATCH alerts 0907-0911 (7 September) and 0909-1156 (9 September) \u2014 single-item alerts, both opinion pieces"
    },
    {
      "ts": "2026-09-09T10:42:14.484039+00:00",
      "text": "A recent WATCH-level flag was wrong: on 8 September, an arts interview about a documentary film on the 1970s Soviet Union was flagged as a Baltic security signal. Cause: a scoring bug. An ambiguous keyword \u2014 one that only counts as a military signal when military vocabulary appears alongside it \u2014 was unlocked by words almost 7,000 characters away, in an unrelated part of a very long article. Combined with a passing mention of Lithuania, that was enough to clear the relevance bar. Related words must now sit close together to count. Checked against 1,581 live items across every direct source: no genuine signal was lost.",
      "context": "WATCH alert 0908-1224, 8 September 2026 12:24 UTC \u2014 sole item from Novaya Gazeta Europe"
    },
    {
